---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import EditorialBlock from '../components/EditorialBlock.astro';
import { SITE_NAME } from '../config/site';
import { tools } from '../config/tools';
import { toolReferences } from '../config/references';
import { accuracyCases, runAccuracyCase } from '../lib/accuracy';
import { dataUpdated, sourceStatuses, sourcesLastChecked, fmtDate } from '../lib/data-status';
import { taxIndia } from '../config/tax-india';
import { taxUS } from '../config/tax-us';
import { gstIndia } from '../config/gst-india';
import { money } from '../lib/format';

const fmt = (v: number, unit: 'usd' | 'inr' | 'pct') => (unit === 'inr' ? money(v, 'INR', v % 1 ? 2 : 0) : money(v, 'USD', v % 1 ? 2 : 0));
const checks = accuracyCases.map((c) => ({ c, ...runAccuracyCase(c) }));
---
<PageLayout title="Methodology" description={`How ${SITE_NAME} calculators work, where their figures come from, how often the data is checked and how errors are corrected.`} path="/methodology/" showUpdated={false}>
  <p>This page explains how every calculator is built and checked, so you can judge how far to trust a result. The short version: formulas are standard and shown on each page, tax figures come from official sources and live in dated config files, and every result is an estimate.</p>

  <h2>Principles</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Formulas in the open.</strong> Each page states its formula and shows a worked example generated by the same code that powers the calculator, so the example cannot disagree with the tool.</li>
    <li><strong>Data separated from code.</strong> Tax slabs, rates and thresholds are not typed into calculators. They sit in configuration files with a source link and a last-updated date.</li>
    <li><strong>Human review.</strong> Figures change only when a person checks the official source and edits the config. Automated checks only raise a flag.</li>
    <li><strong>Honest limits.</strong> Each page lists its assumptions. Where a figure could not be confirmed on an official source, we say so.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How the data is checked</h2>
  <p>Tax data was last updated on <strong>{fmtDate(dataUpdated.any)}</strong> (India income tax {fmtDate(dataUpdated.india)}, US tax {fmtDate(dataUpdated.us)}, GST {fmtDate(dataUpdated.gst)}). An automated job runs weekly, fetches the official pages below, and opens an issue for a person if a page changed, a known figure disappeared or a page could not be reached repeatedly. It never edits figures itself. Last run: {fmtDate(sourcesLastChecked)}. Official websites sometimes block automated requests, so a page can show as unreachable; that is reported rather than hidden. None of these sources provides a machine-readable feed, so the check detects page changes rather than reading numbers.</p>
  <ul>
    {sourceStatuses.map((s) => <li><a href={s.url} rel="noopener">{s.title}</a>: {s.state === 'ok' ? 'unchanged since last review' : s.state}{s.lastChecked && ` (checked ${fmtDate(s.lastChecked)})`}</li>)}
  </ul>
  <h3>Verification status today</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>India income tax:</strong> {taxIndia.verificationNote}</li>
    <li><strong>US tax:</strong> {taxUS.verificationNote}</li>
    <li><strong>GST:</strong> {gstIndia.verificationNote}</li>
  </ul>
  <p>See the <a href="/changelog/">data changelog</a> for every change and the <a href="/corrections/">corrections policy</a> for how mistakes are handled.</p>

  <h2>Accuracy checks against published figures</h2>
  <p>Our automated tests compare calculator output with figures published by official bodies. Rows marked "reproduction" use a copy of an official document because we could not retrieve the original. These run on every change, and the numbers below were computed when this page was built.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="figure-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Check</th><th scope="col">Published</th><th scope="col">This site</th><th scope="col">Result</th><th scope="col">Source</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {checks.map(({ c, ours, pass }) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">{c.title}</th>
            <td>{fmt(c.published, c.unit)}</td>
            <td>{fmt(ours, c.unit)}</td>
            <td>{pass ? 'Match' : 'Mismatch'}</td>
            <td><a href={c.source.url} rel="noopener">{c.source.label}</a> {c.source.kind === 'reproduction' && <span class="tag">Reproduction</span>}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Loan, SIP and savings-goal results are also cross-checked against an independent month-by-month computation in the tests. We have not compared them with any particular bank's calculator, because lenders round and charge fees differently.</p>

  <h2>How each calculator works</h2>
  {tools.map((t) => (
    <section id={t.slug} class="method-tool">
      <h3><a href={`/${t.slug}/`}>{t.shortTitle}</a></h3>
      <pre class="formula">{t.formula}</pre>
      <p><strong>Assumptions:</strong> {t.assumptions.join(' ')}</p>
      {toolReferences(t.slug).length > 0 && <p><strong>References:</strong> {toolReferences(t.slug).map((r, i) => <><a href={r.url} rel="noopener">{r.label}</a>{i < toolReferences(t.slug).length - 1 && '; '}</>)}</p>}
    </section>
  ))}

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <p>Results are estimates for planning. They are not financial, tax or legal advice (see the <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a>). Real outcomes depend on fees, rounding, your full tax position and rules that change after the date shown on each page.</p>

  <EditorialBlock />
</PageLayout>
